WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:08.470
A reviewer will take your analysis, start a clean session on a different machine, and run it again.

2
00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:11.330
If any number changes, the result is in doubt.

3
00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:13.010
Reproducibility is not a feeling.

4
00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:17.890
It is a property you can test: same inputs, same code, same outputs, byte for byte.

5
00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:22.070
Random numbers come from a generator with internal state.

6
00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:25.890
set.seed() fixes that state, so the same seed gives the same draws.

7
00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:32.290
Sampling three values from 1 to 10 with seed 1 gives 9, 4, and 7, every time.

8
00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:35.700
Without a seed, every run of a bootstrap gives a slightly different interval.

9
00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:39.200
But a seed alone is not enough.

10
00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:44.390
In version 3.6, R changed how sample() turns random numbers into indices.

11
00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:48.530
With the old "Rounding" method, the same seed gives 3, 4, and 5 instead.

12
00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:52.980
Any session, script, or package can change these settings, and your results change with them.

13
00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:55.660
So pin everything.

14
00:00:55.660 --> 00:01:01.850
Pass kind, normal.kind, and sample.kind to set.seed(), and the draws no longer depend on the caller.

15
00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:03.990
Then put the caller's state back.

16
00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:11.750
withr::with_seed() does both: it sets the seed and kinds, runs your code, and restores the previous random stream when it finishes.

17
00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:15.770
The second habit is computed reporting.

18
00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:20.290
A number typed into a report is a copy, and copies drift when the data changes.

19
00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:25.930
In a Quarto or R Markdown report, inline code computes each number as the document renders.

20
00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:29.290
The sentence in the report and the analysis can never disagree.

21
00:01:31.130 --> 00:01:32.930
Write a function that builds the sentence from data.

22
00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:38.490
For each arm, take the non-missing values, compute the mean and a t-interval, and format with sprintf() to two decimals.

23
00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:47.400
For our synthetic Week 12 data, Placebo shows a mean change of 2.19, with 25 participants, all computed.

24
00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:50.560
Now the full analysis.

25
00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:54.590
The analysis set keeps participants with a Week 12 value and computes change from baseline.

26
00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:58.190
The primary model is lm(change ~ arm + baseline).

27
00:01:58.190 --> 00:02:05.820
In our synthetic trial, 72 participants qualify, and treatment lowers the change by about 3.5 points.

28
00:02:07.730 --> 00:02:17.080
Every output goes to a file: the results table, the audit sample of participants for source data checks, and a provenance note with the R version, the seed, and a checksum of the input.

29
00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:20.360
Run it twice, and the files are byte for byte identical.

30
00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:24.080
A timestamp or an unpinned random draw would break that instantly.

31
00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:27.560
That is the reviewer's test.

32
00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:33.860
Re-run from another directory, with different options and generator settings, then compare checksums of every output file.

33
00:02:34.210 --> 00:02:40.540
When they match, the analysis is reproducible, and anyone can trace each number back to the code and data that produced it.

34
00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:44.380
Your final missions follow this path.

35
00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:48.550
Make a bootstrap reproducible under any generator settings, without disturbing the caller.

36
00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:51.510
Generate report text where every number is computed.

37
00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:57.410
Then the final boss: an analysis whose output files a reviewer re-creates exactly, from a clean session.
